ped setting record lows","change":"Monroe's nights have warmed. Its days have warmed since 1986.","rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 61\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>61\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 68\u00b0F, and the record is 88\u00b0F (1955). The low of <strong>38\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Monroe has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Monroe's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Monroe has set <strong class=\"sig\">43 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 88 that chance alone would give, and <strong>104 record highs</strong>, close to the 88 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (57). Monroe's all-time high is 109\u00b0F, on July 26, 1918.","t_chg":"Monroe's nights have warmed. Its days have warmed since 1986.","p_chg":"Across 107 complete years: Monroe's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.11\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.04 to +0.18), about +1.5\u00b0F since 1894. Monroe's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.06\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.15 to +0.03) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.49\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.17 to +0.81) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.66\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.31 to +1.01). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 3 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; January fell.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Monroe's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.38 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 55.0 inches a year. The wettest year was 1920 (79.56 in); the driest, 2005 (31.13 in). Monroe almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.6 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 11 daily record highs, 2 daily record lows"}}